  "title": "Planets around two stars: where can an orbit survive?",
  "subtitle": "5 steps of Planets in Binary Stars (37 in the full investigation)",
  "body": "<p class=\"inv-step-count\">Step 2 of 5 <span class=\"inv-step-kind\">predict</span></p><h3 class=\"inv-step-title\">Move it out</h3><div class=\"inv-step-body\"><p>Now double the planet's starting radius, to 0.30 of the separation — 3 AU. That is still less than half the closest the two stars ever come to each other, so the planet is nowhere near either of them at the start.</p></div><p class=\"inv-prompt\" id=\"invChoicePrompt\">At 0.30 separations, over twenty binary periods, you expect:</p><div class=\"inv-options\" role=\"radiogroup\" aria-labelledby=\"invChoicePrompt\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"0\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">A</span><span>Much the same as before — a slightly wider, slightly wobblier ring</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"1\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">B</span><span>A ring that visibly breathes but stays put</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"2\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">C</span><span>The planet to leave</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"3\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">D</span><span>The planet to fall into one of the stars</span></button></div>",
  "previousDisabled": false,
  "nextDisabled": false,
  "depthState": ""
 },
