 },
 {
  "role": "last",
  "sid": "ejected-means-what",
  "position": [
   5,
   5
  ],
  "title": "Planets around two stars: where can an orbit survive?",
  "subtitle": "5 steps of Planets in Binary Stars (37 in the full investigation)",
  "body": "<p class=\"inv-step-count\">Step 5 of 5 <span class=\"inv-step-kind\">question</span></p><h3 class=\"inv-step-title\">What does \"ejected\" mean here?</h3><div class=\"inv-step-body\"><p>The panel called that an ejection. It has two conditions for the word and it needs both: the planet must have positive energy with respect to <em>both</em> stars, and it must be more than ten binary separations away.</p></div><p class=\"inv-prompt\" id=\"invChoicePrompt\">Why is positive energy on its own not enough?</p><div class=\"inv-options\" role=\"radiogroup\" aria-labelledby=\"invChoicePrompt\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"0\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">A</span><span>Because energy is not conserved well enough to trust</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"1\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">B</span><span>Because a planet can be briefly unbound during an encounter and still come back</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"2\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">C</span><span>Because the planet’s mass is too small for energy to mean anything</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"inv-option\" data-option=\"3\" role=\"radio\" aria-checked=\"false\"><span class=\"inv-option-mark\">D</span><span>Because the barycenter moves, so energy is measured in the wrong frame</span></button></div>",
  "previousDisabled": false,
  "nextDisabled": false,
  "depthState": ""
 }
